The reduct of a programme Πw.r.t. an interpretation T, denoted ΠT, is obtained by
(1) removing rules with a default literal not L in the body if L∈T, and (2) removing
default literals from remaining rules. An interpretation Tis an answer set ofΠif it is
minimal (under set inclusion) among all interpretations satisfying ΠT.
Aggregates. ASP allows for the direct use of aggregates, which are elaborately de-
scribed in Chapter 7 of [10]. Speciﬁcally, the aggregate # sum is useful for our applica-
tion, as it allows us to sum all values of the properties of a parent. This results in a ﬁnal
score for each parent, determining the choice of the judge.
Multiple Answer Sets. The output of an ASP programme is allminimal answer sets
that are consistent with the rules given by the programme. In our approach, each an-
swer set includes a possible numeric weight for each property, and thus each answer setrepresents a possible explanation of the decision of the judge.
Non-monotonicity. ASP follows a non-monotonic approach to reasoning, allowing
the representation of incomplete information, see e.g. [12]. This allows conclusions to
change as new information is added. For example, a choice to grant custody to a mothercan result in a choice for the father when new properties are added to the case. Speciﬁ-cally, previous explanations are dismissed when more predicates are added to the case.
Use of Python Our approach builds on the multiple answer sets that an ASP pro-
gramme outputs. In some cases, the number of answer sets can be very high, which
requires a script to analyze the output. We do this using Python, described in Sec. 3.
Remark 1 The full code used to produce the results in this paper can be found on the
following github repository: https://github.com/josephinedik/JURIX2025.git. All results
were generated using clingo version 5.4.0 (see [13]) and Python version 3.6.7.
3. Implementation
Our implementation can be found in Table 1. The predicates used are the following:
•props(X) denotes that Xis a property;
•parent(P) denotes that Pis a parent;
•hasProp(P ,X) denotes that parent Phas property X;
•obl(X) resp. perm(X) denotes that a property Xis obligatory resp. permitted;
•goodness(X) resp. badness(X) denotes that property Xis good resp. bad;
•permPlus(X) resp. permMin(X) resp. permZero(X) denotes that Xis a positively
resp. negatively resp. neutrally permitted property;
•num(N) denotes that Nis a natural number;
•weight(X,N) denotes that Xhas a weight of Nwhere Nis a natural number;
•values(X,N) denotes that Xhas a value of Nwhere Nis an integer;
•choose(P) denotes that parent Pwas chosen by the judge;
•points(P ,N) denotes that parent Phas Npoints;
•category(C) denotes that Cis a category;
•catProp(X,C) denotes that a property Xhas category C.
